# Rendered by paisans. Do not edit: `paisans apply` overwrites this file.
#
# Named Caddy snippets for gate, imported by NAME
# (`import gate_provisional`, `import gate_members`), never by path: nothing
# renders a host block for this file, and it carries no routing of its own
# until some other site's snippet imports one of these names inside its own
# final `handle` block.
#
# gate_provisional never refuses a request: it only checks whether someone is
# signed in at all, which is what this kind's own onboarding surface needs.
# gate_members is the enforcing one, and it is the only one that should ever
# gate a member-facing application - it answers 403 for anyone signed in who
# is not yet a member, which caddy.snippet.tmpl's compose file arranges by
# giving only `members` the OAUTH2_PROXY_ALLOWED_GROUPS restriction. Using
# gate_provisional to protect an application would admit every signed-in
# visitor, provisional or not, which is the exact mistake this two-instance
# shape exists to make impossible.
#
# Modelled on (pocketid_gate) in rsync/caddy/Caddyfile, paisans.community: the
# deployment this kind generalises defines one snippet gating on a single
# enforcing instance. Here that becomes two named snippets, because this
# toolkit's onboarding surface (a code-of-conduct style page, gated on
# "signed in" rather than "member") is exactly the case that instance split
# exists for.
#
# STRIP BEFORE FORWARDING. Whatever consumes X-Auth-Request-User must trust
# nothing upstream of Caddy: a client reaching this gate directly, bypassing
# Caddy, could forge the header on its way in. `request_header` (not `header`,
# which edits the response) removes it from the inbound request before
# forward_auth's subrequest can see it; copy_headers below only ever adds the
# verified header back, so it does nothing to remove one already forged.
(gate_provisional) {
	route {
		request_header -X-Auth-Request-*
		forward_auth 10.44.0.1:4180 {
			uri /oauth2/auth
			copy_headers X-Auth-Request-User X-Auth-Request-Email X-Auth-Request-Preferred-Username
		}
	}
}

(gate_members) {
	route {
		request_header -X-Auth-Request-*
		forward_auth 10.44.0.1:4181 {
			uri /oauth2/auth
			copy_headers X-Auth-Request-User X-Auth-Request-Email X-Auth-Request-Preferred-Username
		}
	}
}
